            IsPublished = true,
            SortOrder = 1,
            AppRoutes = """["/inventory/replenishment","/parts"]""",
            Tags = """["planning","replenishment","safety-stock","reorder-point","inventory"]""",
            ContentJson = """{"body":"## Safety Stock and Reorder Points\n\nMRP plans the parts that show up on a bill of materials for a scheduled job. Plenty of what you buy does not work that way: fasteners, consumables, packaging, shop supplies and any stocked part you keep on the shelf regardless of what is scheduled. For those, you want a simple rule — *when it drops to this level, buy this much* — and someone watching it every day so nothing runs out.\n\nThat is what **Safety stock + reorder point** does. You set a few numbers on each part, Forge checks every part overnight, and anything that has fallen too low shows up as a **reorder suggestion** on the **Replenishment** tab of Inventory. Approve a suggestion and Forge writes a draft purchase order for you.\n\nThe capability is off until an admin turns it on. Once it is on, the Replenishment tab appears in Inventory and the nightly check starts running.\n\n### The four numbers on a part\n\nOpen a part and look at its **Inventory** cluster. Four fields drive replenishment:\n\n- **Reorder Point** — the stock level that triggers a suggestion. When available stock *plus what is already on order* is at or below this number, the part needs reordering. If you set a reorder point, it wins over every other rule.\n- **Reorder Quantity** — how much to buy each time. If you set it, the suggestion uses it as-is. If you leave it blank, Forge sizes the order from how fast you are using the part.\n- **Safety Stock Days** — how many days of cushion you want beyond the vendor's lead time. Only used when no reorder point is set, or when Forge has to size the order for you.\n- **Min Stock** — the fallback threshold. If the part has no reorder point and no usage history, the part needs reordering when available stock is at or below this number.\n\nYou do not need all four on every part. A reorder point and a reorder quantity are enough for a part you know well. For a part you want Forge to watch on its own, leave those blank and set safety stock days instead.\n\n### How the nightly check decides\n\nOnce a day, early in the morning, Forge looks at every part and works out its **burn rate** — average units consumed per day, based on picks and shipments over the last 30, 60 and 90 days. It uses the longest window that has any history.\n\nThen it asks, in this order:\n\n1. **Is there a reorder point?** If available stock plus open purchase orders is at or below it, the part needs reordering.\n2. **Otherwise, is the part being consumed?** If so, it works out how many units you burn during the vendor's lead time plus your safety stock days (the lead time comes from the part's sourcing setup; when nothing is set, Forge assumes 14 days of lead time and 7 days of safety stock). If available plus on-order stock is less than that, the part needs reordering.\n3. **Otherwise, is there a min stock?** If available stock is at or below it, the part needs reordering.\n\nA part with none of those numbers and no usage history is left alone.\n\nWhen a part needs reordering, Forge creates one pending suggestion for it with a **suggested quantity**: the part's reorder quantity if you set one; otherwise the burn rate times (lead time plus safety stock days, with 14 days assumed for each when unset); otherwise the min stock amount. It also estimates **days of stock remaining** and a **projected stockout date** from the burn rate. A part already has a pending suggestion is not suggested twice, and a pending suggestion that no longer applies — because stock arrived — is expired automatically on the next run. Admins and managers get a notification when new suggestions are ready.\n\n### The Replenishment tab\n\n**Inventory > Replenishment** has two sections.\n\n**Pending Reorder Suggestions** is your queue. Each row shows the part, its preferred vendor, available stock, burn per day, days left, projected stockout and the suggested quantity. Days left is color coded: red at a week or less, amber at three weeks or less. The tab itself shows a badge with the pending count so you can see from anywhere in Inventory whether there is work waiting.\n\n**Burn Rate Analysis** is the part list underneath: every part with its 30-, 60- and 90-day burn rates, on-hand and on-order quantities, days left and a **Reorder** flag. Use the search box to find a part, and the **Needs Reorder** filter to narrow the list to parts that are flagged. This is where you go to sanity-check a suggestion or to spot a part that is trending toward trouble before the nightly check flags it.\n\n### Approve or dismiss\n\n**Approve** on a suggestion creates a **draft purchase order** to the part's preferred vendor for the suggested quantity, with a note tying it back to the suggestion. The PO is a draft with no price filled in — open it under Purchase Orders, confirm the quantity and price, and submit it the way you would any other PO. Select several suggestions and **Approve Selected** creates one PO per suggestion in one go.\n\nA part with no preferred vendor cannot be approved. Set the vendor on the part first, then approve.\n\n**Dismiss** (the X) closes a suggestion without ordering. You must give a reason — *already covered by job stock*, *part being retired*, *vendor on hold* — so the decision is on record. The part will be evaluated again on the next nightly run, so a dismissed part comes back if it is still low.\n\n### Tuning\n\n- Suggestions coming too late? Raise the reorder point, or add safety stock days on parts that rely on the burn-rate rule.\n- Suggestions coming for parts you buy against jobs? Those parts belong to MRP; clear their reorder point and min stock so the nightly check leaves them alone.\n- Suggested quantities too small or too large? Set an explicit reorder quantity that matches the vendor's pack size or price break.\n- Burn rates showing a dash? The part has no picks or shipments in the last 90 days. Forge cannot size an order from usage, so give it a reorder point and reorder quantity.\n\n### What to do first\n\n1. Ask an admin to turn on **Safety stock + reorder point**.\n2. Pick your ten most painful stockout parts. On each, set a reorder point, a reorder quantity and a preferred vendor.\n3. Tomorrow, open **Inventory > Replenishment** and review the queue.\n4. Approve what makes sense, dismiss the rest with a reason, and finish the draft POs under Purchase Orders.\n5. Once a week, scan Burn Rate Analysis with the Needs Reorder filter on and adjust part settings that keep producing surprises.","sections":[]}"""
        });

